{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1788297714,"nonce":"UEUVuvpOXGb8WewBX69g75HksCQ=","target":"938612546bcdbce14680285eef8062d7c6cbef8d0a69df88e03a21d88242642c","message":"Shipped on `spec/posture` (`a83db72`, `d269b53`, `026b8e3`, forked from `c429dda`). `dotnet build -c Release`: **0 warnings, 0 errors**. The branch carries NO assembly — `git checkout -- Assemblies/` after the build, per the round contract. **Nothing here has met a bench.**\n\n## GREPPED FIRST — what was already shipped\n\n- `SeekVerbs.cs` already emits `will_seek` per pawn, via `SeekMod.WillSeek` -\u003e `SeekRegistry.ShouldSeek`, published in `SeekStateOf` and echoed nowhere else. The per-pawn half of this issue's digest ask existed; the ROLLUP did not, and neither did the area or hostility halves.\n- `assign` already owns the area lever (`AreaGate`, reproducing `PawnColumnWorker_AllowedArea.DoCell` + `Area.AssignableAsAllowed`) and the hostility lever (`PawnColumnWorker_HostilityResponse.DoCell` + the dropdown's Attack omission). `posture` **calls** `AreaGate` rather than copying it.\n- `seek-at-will` already implements the seek gate by CALLING `Patch_PawnGetGizmos.ShowsSeekGizmo`, with `SeekGateReason`/`SeekGateText` for which clause refused. Reused unchanged.\n- `area allowed create` / `area allowed add` already ship. `posture` does not duplicate them, and that is load-bearing — see the refusal below.\n- Verb registration is an attribute scan (`VerbRegistry`), so `posture` needed no registry edit and no other worker's file was touched.\n\n## THE PREMISE OF THIS ISSUE IS WRONG, and the evidence it cites is the refutation\n\nThe issue (and the lesson) say the `hostility_response` echo is truthful but misleading because SeekAndKill's node sits above `ThinkNode_ConditionalColonist` and makes the vanilla flee node unreachable. Checked against `/home/dorian/projects/rimworld/seekandkill/Source/SeekAndKill/` and the decompiled 1.6 tree, not assumed:\n\n1. **`JobGiver_ConfigurableHostilityResponse` is not in the `Humanlike` tree at all.** It is in **`HumanlikeConstant`**, under `ThinkNode_ConditionalCanDoConstantThinkTreeJobNow` (`Data/Core/Defs/ThinkTreeDefs/Humanlike.xml`). It is the only producer of `JobDefOf.FleeAndCower` for a sane colonist — the other two are `JobGiver_FleeAllPawns` (mental states) and the colonist-bar drawer.\n2. **SeekAndKill never injects into that tree.** `ThinkTreeInjector.Inject` needs one of four anchors at the ROOT — `ThinkNode_ConditionalColonist`, `ThinkNode_QueuedJob`, a LordDuty `ThinkNode_Subtree`, `ThinkNode_ConditionalRevenantState` — and `HumanlikeConstant`'s root holds exactly `ThinkNode_Subtree(Despawned)`, that conditional, and `ThinkNode_ConditionalCanDoLordJobNow`. None match; the loop `continue`s.\n3. **The constant tree runs FIRST and pre-empts.** `Verse.AI/Pawn_JobTracker.DetermineNextJob` calls `DetermineNextConstantThinkTreeJob()` and returns its result without ever touching `MainThinkNodeRoot`; `JobTrackerTickInterval` re-runs it every 30 ticks (`RimWorld/AITuning.ConstantThinkTreeJobCheckIntervalTicks`) and starts its job with `JobCondition.InterruptForced` over whatever is running — including a `JobGiver_SquadSeek` job.\n4. **A second consumer says the same thing one level down.** `JobGiver_ReactToCloseMeleeThreat` is at index 6 of the `Humanlike` root, above the index-11 insertion point (the first `ThinkNode_ConditionalColonist` is index 11, the \"wait if drafted\" one), and it returns null unless `hostilityResponse == Attack`.\n\n**Op 109 is the proof, not the illustration.** Seek ON, hostility `Flee`, Captain in `JobDriver_FleeAndCower` — impossible if the flee node is unreachable. `Flee` beat seek, exactly as the decision order says it must.\n\nConsequences, all now in code and in DESIGN:\n\n- **`hostility:\"Attack\"` is the load-bearing setting, not a backstop.** Seek is what happens after the close-range node declines.\n- **Seek ON + `Flee` is the worst combination available**: run from anything within 8 cells with LOS (`FleeUtility.ShouldFleeFrom`, `checkDistance:true`), march at anything beyond it. That is M1 day 1 and M1 day 4 in one state, and `digest.posture.flee_risk` names every pawn in it.\n- **`on_contact` CAN be published honestly**, because the order is deterministic and every input is already published. It is not a guess and it is not a disagreement — it is the resolution.\n\n## `on_contact`\n\nEight verdicts, closed vocabulary, first match wins, each carrying the member that decides it: `downed` (Downed subtree at root index 2, and the injector skips that tree) · `mental-break` (`ShouldSeek` and the constant gate both require `!InMentalState`) · `player-controlled` (both require `!Drafted`) · `flee` · `attack-then-seek` · `attack-nearby` (radius 8 melee, else `Clamp(EffectiveRange*0.66, 2, 20)`) · `seek-only` · `ignore`.\n\n**Every verdict is published at every read, zero included.** A count that appeared only when non-zero would make `posture.on_contact.flee` a path that resolves today and refuses tomorrow, and session 19 ruled an unresolvable path a refusal at arm time.\n\n**What it deliberately does NOT model, said rather than implied**: asleep (`ThinkNode_ConditionalLyingDown` at root index 0; the constant gate needs `pawn.Awake()`) and mid-forced-job (`PawnUtility.PlayerForcedJobNowOrSoon`). Both are transient — a field that flickers with the day/night cycle is not a posture — and contact wakes a sleeper first, after which the verdict applies.\n\n## The verb\n\n`posture {area, pawns?, seek?, hostility?, dry_run?, allow_empty_area?}`\n\n- **No lever at all is a pure READ** — `seek-at-will`'s contract. `posture {}` answers \"what is our posture\" and mutates nothing.\n- **Any lever requires `area`**, because a posture with two of three settings is the defect. `area:null` DECLARES unrestricted, so it is a recorded choice rather than an omission (the `threat-pardon` precedent).\n- `seek` is `true` (default) | `false` | `\"auto\"`; `\"auto\"` reuses the shipped `AutoOn` skill rule, which is what would have kept three unarmed colonists home on M1 day 1. `hostility` defaults `\"Attack\"`.\n- **An absent area is REFUSED, not created.** Decisive reason: a fresh `Area_Allowed` is EMPTY and `RimWorld/ForbidUtility.InAllowedArea` short-circuits on `TrueCount \u003e 0`, so an auto-created area binds nothing while the verb would report every pawn bound — the fix manufacturing the exact false report this issue exists to remove. Two lesser reasons: `new Area_Allowed(...)` rolls `Rand.Value` twice (determinism class R), and `area allowed create`/`add` already ship. The same short-circuit makes a NAMED zero-cell area a bad-args refusal unless `allow_empty_area:true`, and makes `area_bound` count a pawn as bound only when its EFFECTIVE area has cells.\n- Per pawn: `applied`, `refused` (each `{lever, reason}`), `before`/`after` with all three settings read BACK, `on_contact` + `on_contact_why`, `violence_capable`. **A pawn incapable of violence gets a headline entry (`incapable_of_violence`, the shape `work_coverage.under` has) AND its own row** — hostility refused by name, seek refused by name, and the area still applying, because \"bound but will never fight\" is the honest outcome.\n- `EffectiveAreaRestrictionInPawnCurrentMap` is guarded on `MapHeld != null` rather than caught: PawnSafe class D, and a swallowed throw would report \"no area\" for a pawn that has one.\n\n## `digest.posture`\n\n`{ok, will_seek, will_seek_n, will_seek_of, area_bound, area_bound_n, area_bound_of, attack, attack_n, attack_of, colonists, areas, on_contact, flee_risk, seek_mod, seek_mod_missing, denominators, note}`.\n\n**n/m ships as a string AND as integers.** The issue asks for `n/m`, which is the glance; a predicate cannot use it, because `advance {until:{condition}}` refuses `\u003c` on a string rather than coercing it (fc287ba 0.6g). Denominators differ on purpose and are published in words: `will_seek`/`attack` over violence-capable free colonists (the game refuses both levers to the others), `area_bound` over those whose `SupportsAllowedAreas` is true.\n\n**Registered as a predicate section — CHEAP on session 19's axis**: no `Room.Role`, no `GetStatValueAbstract`, no pathfind. One `FreeColonistsSpawned` snapshot and, per pawn, field reads, one dictionary lookup, a `GetLord()` walk, a bitmask combine, and three cached-`MethodInfo` invokes whose bodies are seven field reads and a HashSet lookup. `Pawn.CombinedDisabledWorkTags` and `Pawn_StoryTracker.DisabledWorkTagsBackstoryTraitsAndGenes` both recompute and write no cache — which is why the violence test is the TAG one and not `GetDisabledWorkTypes`, whose getter fills `cachedDisabledWorkTypes`. `posture.ok == false` is the halt the M1 post-mortem asks for.\n\n## Save/load — READ, not assumed. All three persist, by three different mechanisms\n\n- **hostility** — `RimWorld/Pawn_PlayerSettings.ExposeData`, `Scribe_Values.Look(ref hostilityResponse, \"hostilityResponse\", HostilityResponseMode.Flee)`. That default is also the field initialiser, so **a fresh colony is a colony of fleers**.\n- **area** — same method, `Scribe_Collections.Look(ref allowedAreas, \"allowedAreas\", LookMode.Reference, LookMode.Reference, …)`, with a save-time prune of null entries and a PostLoadInit clear for Roamers.\n- **seek** — the one in doubt, and it survives: `SeekAndKillGameComponent.ExposeData` scribes the set as `SK_SeekPawns`, **but only when `PSInterop.PsToggleShared` is false** — with Perspective Shift present, PS's `seekAtWillPawns` is the single source of truth and S\u0026K writes nothing. PS is OUT of the bench modlist, so on this bench the set is S\u0026K's own. `LoadedGame` then runs `PruneStaleIds`, which drops only ids absent from `PawnsFinder.AllMapsWorldAndTemporary_Alive` and bails entirely when `Find.Maps.Count == 0`, so a living colonist is never pruned.\n\nThe bullet's \"or the digest says it did not\" is met by construction: every figure is counted from LIVE state at each glance, `seek_mod`/`seek_mod_missing` name a mod that stopped answering, and `flee_risk` names anyone whose response has fallen back to `Flee`.\n\n## Docs\n\n- `checklists/triggered.md` item 12 is now \"confirm the posture verb ran\" — paint the area, one `posture` call, verdict is `digest.posture.ok`.\n- `[[seek-off-is-a-decision-to-flee]]` keeps the why and the policy choice, **with its mechanism half corrected on the way out** rather than deleted — the note now says plainly that its old claim was wrong in the direction that killed the colony. `playbook/INDEX.md`'s one-line summary updated with it.\n- Three DESIGN decisions-log entries: the mechanism and what `on_contact` can honestly say; the verb's shape and the refusal reasoning; what persists across save/load.\n\n## Acceptance status, bullet by bullet\n\n| Bullet | Status |\n|---|---|\n| One verb sets all three, reports per pawn what it did and refused | **covered** — suite phase 2 (every row publishes both lists; every refusal names lever AND reason; `after` read back) |\n| Pawn incapable of violence refused BY NAME | **covered, FIXTURE-GATED** — phase 3; the fixture cannot be forced (below) |\n| `digest` publishes `posture` with `will_seek`/`area_bound` as n/m | **covered** — phase 0 shape contract + phase 4 |\n| Survives save/load, or the digest says it did not | **orchestrator-manual** — phases 7/8, split by a human save+load, with a `session {kind:\"loaded\"}` row proved FIRST |\n| `checklists/` item 12 shrinks | **covered** — doc |\n| Lesson keeps only the WHY | **covered** — doc |\n\n`accept/b1b3060-posture.py` (+ `.md`): 11 phases, 117 expectations in the default sweep. `--selftest` passes **26/26 offline** and it found a real defect in the file — `show()` threw on a `set`, which would have turned one red check into a traceback and lost the exit code. `--dry-run` prints the plan and asserts nothing, and says so.\n\n## The one thing I could not build a deterministic fixture for — filed as `ff1f0b9`\n\n**`dev:spawn-pawn {violence_capable:false}` does NOT make a nonviolent pawn.** The flag is `PawnGenerationRequest.mustBeCapableOfViolence`, so `false` only stops REQUIRING capability. Checked against the shipped Defs: no vanilla trait disables `Violent` (the three that mention it list it under `requiredWorkTags`), no vanilla hediff stage does, `PawnKindDef.disabledWorkTags` carries it only on Anomaly entities, and the one deterministic lever is Biotech's `ViolenceDisabled` gene — which **no shipped verb can apply**. So phase 3 rolls colonists in batches of ten up to forty and SKIPS with exit 2 and the recipe rather than asserting against luck. Filed `ff1f0b9` (p2, wave:3) for `dev:add-gene`/`dev:remove-gene`, with an Acceptance section.\n\n## Fixture recipe for the bench run\n\nBench + loaded colony, paused, plus: open buildable ground for a 12×12 (the suite creates and paints `acc-posture` itself, and creates an empty `acc-posture-empty` for the zero-cell refusal — both left behind, delete them if the map nears the ten-area cap); **at least two colonists**; SeekAndKill loaded (the suite reads `posture.seek_mod` and does not assume — without it, seek refusals are checked to name the mod instead); and for phase 3, a violence-incapable colonist — either let the phase roll for one (**it leaves up to forty extra colonists on the map**) or hand-build a colony at world-gen where the character editor shows \"Incapable of: Violent\", which is the repeatable route until `ff1f0b9` lands.\n\nPhases 6/7/8 are opt-in. **6 spawns a raid** and is the empirical half of the mechanism finding above; it uses a bounded `advance {ticks:2500}` with the reason written down, because no predicate expresses \"this pawn is fleeing\" — the ops are `\u003c \u003c= \u003e \u003e= == !=` and `colonists.list[*].job` is a truncated driver report string. Every `advance` in the file goes through one helper carrying `722c951`'s `unread_ok` and `through_casualties`, both naming this suite.\n\n**Nothing BLOCKED.** No file outside the lane was touched.","files":null}]}